Cell["\<\
This section is for calculating the shape of the observed image, based on the \
perturbative method used in the original paper.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.953557283506886*^9, 3.9535573208625917`*^9}, {
  3.9535574011357603`*^9, 
  3.95355742064298*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"7aace178-7d8d-4b02-b633-\
f89296c89026"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Evaluate the \[ScriptCapitalM] matrix", "Subsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.933812393894965*^9, 3.9338124083886347`*^9}, {
  3.933812486423716*^9, 
  3.933812486868862*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"a50c7263-8cd0-4367-91ab-\
36d5de3476ee"],

Cell["\<\
Here we use both semi-analytic and numeric methods to evaluate the \
\[ScriptCapitalM] matrix characterizing the map between the source position \
and the image plane.

The semi-analytic expressions are derived by working out the relevant partial \
derivatives directly from the integral formalism, including properly treating \
the integrand and possible turning points. The results are expressed as \
definite integrals that will be evaluated numerically, so this method is \
dubbed as \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]semi-analytic\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote]. The \
process of deriving it is quite lengthy and will not be shown here.

On the other hand, the numeric method is much more straightforward\[LongDash]\
\[LongDash]numeric derivative by introducing a small perturbation \[Epsilon]  \
to the parameters. We have checked that the two methods lead to consistent \
results.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9338252607564297`*^9, 3.933825506978609*^9}, {
  3.933825547804968*^9, 
  3.933825999356365*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"34923f4a-aebc-430c-8465-\
bbb708b1a26c"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Semi-analytic derivatives", "Subsubsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.933812345992029*^9, 3.933812360003571*^9}, {
  3.933818009806231*^9, 3.93381801329235*^9}, {3.933819042435493*^9, 
  3.9338190611067553`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"b1f19383-5e54-4391-b3c1-\
abec9f12d827"],

