Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Examples", "Section",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.93381353445541*^9, 3.93381353561159*^9}, 
   3.953559247998397*^9},ExpressionUUID->"1b4fabb9-33ca-408e-b90a-\
078eac358e56"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "In this section we present examples to show the multiple functions of this \
Mathematica code.\n\nDue to the high nonlinearity of the geodesic equation, \
it\[CloseCurlyQuote]s recommended to first solve the imaging conditions ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"(", 
    RowBox[{
     SubscriptBox["\[Theta]", "f"], "=", 
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{
       SubscriptBox["\[Theta]", "o"], " ", "and", " ", 
       SubscriptBox["\[Phi]", "f"]}], "\[Congruent]", 
      RowBox[{
       SubscriptBox["\[Phi]", "o"], " ", 
       RowBox[{"(", 
        RowBox[{"mod", " ", "2", "\[Pi]"}], ")"}]}]}]}], ")"}], 
   TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->"1a73ba0a-9d90-4c88-a842-ac2f994cb699"],
 " using contour plots (as described in the original paper using the python \
code in https://github.com/AuroraDysis/KerrP2P ), and then plug the values of \
conserved quantities into this Mathematica code to investigate geodesic \
visualization and image-plane position and shape."
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.953557736204109*^9, 3.9535577705015717`*^9}, {
  3.953557821607121*^9, 3.95355792507136*^9}, {3.95355809354943*^9, 
